The complete result is encoded into canonical bytes that can cross a process or network boundary without losing what it proves.\nnever learns about state.\n\nThe sumcheck and GKR crates do not depend on the sparse-Merkle compiler, and only the primitives crate touches the underlying field and hash libraries. That boundary is what lets another frontend reuse the core.\n\n`statesync-gkr`Facade\n\nThe entry point for host code: requests, preparation, proving, encoding and verification.\n\n`ssgkr-verification`Verification\n\nOwns the composed request and result types and the acceptance path.\n\n`ssgkr-wrap`External proof\n\nExternal proof, manifest, receipt and local transition encodings. Meets verification only at the facade.\n\n`ssgkr-batching`Batching\n\nGroups independent jobs that share a prepared circuit; each job keeps its own proof.\n\n`ssgkr-commitment`Commitment\n\nCircuit and prepared-material commitments shared by verification and the external path.\n\n`ssgkr-compiler`Sparse-Merkle frontend\n\nNative sparse-Merkle semantics compiled to a layered circuit and witness layout.\n\n`ssgkr-protocol`GKR core\n\nLayer-by-layer GKR proving and verification over any layered circuit.\n\n`ssgkr-sumcheck`Sumcheck core\n\nThe sumcheck prover and verifier that every layer reduction runs on.\n\n`ssgkr-primitives`Field and hash\n\nKoalaBear field, degree-four extension, Poseidon2 and challenger, built on Plonky3 0.4.3 components.\n\nListed from the facade down to the primitives. The highlighted crates form the generic proving core. Dependency direction, the sumcheck message order, the circuit shape, the acceptance boundary, the transcript order and the public-input set are frozen for the current release line.\n\n## See the results it produces.\n\nMemory, verification, preparation, parallel batches and sustained delivery, each under its own controlled conditions.\n\nPerformanceFormal verification--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStateSync-GKR: Design and Evaluation of a Reusable Sparse GKR Engine (https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14849)\nciteturn0academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 weeks ago; We present StateSync-GKR, a reusable Rust sumcheck/GKR core with a sparse-Merkle frontend, and connect its design mechanisms to controlled measurements. ... With circuit, field, transcript and prover driver held fixed, sparse reduction lowers additional requested allocation 3,242-fold and peak process resident memory 89.46-fold relative to StateSync-GKR's test-only dense reference for a two-layer mixed circuit of width 4096.\nTitle: StateSync-GKR: Design and Evaluation of a Reusable Sparse GKR Engine\nAuthors: Jinwook Kim\nDate: Sat Oct 10 12:06:57 2026\n\nAuthenticated state transitions need proof engines that conserve memory, avoid repeated setup, and preserve request semantics across parallel execution. We present StateSync-GKR, a reusable Rust sumcheck/GKR core with a sparse-Merkle frontend, and connect its design mechanisms to controlled measurements. Sparse layer reduction and derived wiring reduce prover ownership and repeated verification work. Cube/affine fusion and parallel path constraints keep the circuit schedule and proof-size growth compact. Reusable preparation and indexed parallel jobs amortize setup while maintaining independent results. Three-state records, strict original-request decoding, and separate core/model interfaces expose the application and assurance boundaries. With circuit, field, transcript and prover driver held fixed, sparse reduction lowers additional requested allocation 3,242-fold and peak process resident memory 89.46-fold relative to StateSync-GKR's test-only dense reference for a two-layer mixed circuit of width 4096. Direct depth-24 fresh/prepared measurements show 3.46-4.45-fold lower per-request proving-path latency. CPU and full-proof delivery studies distinguish useful parallel work from queueing and return-path costs, retaining all outcomes and latency budgets. The evaluation supports larger circuits within finite RAM, lower repeated-request cost, and explicit deployment choices for this artifact. It preserves the cryptographic claim boundary of the existing construction.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOraclizer Research | StateSync-GKR Prover: A Design Journey (https://research.oraclizer.io/statesync-gkr-prover-design-journey/)\nciteturn0search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 days ago; Crawled: 3 days ago; 20 Oct 2026 · 20 min read ... We wrote Rust in place of gnark, and in place of another team's GKR library we implemented sumcheck and GKR to their published specifications on open-source field and hash primitives. ... That deadline is set by the slowest request more than by the average, and a GKR prover's time is linear in circuit size and, for a fixed shape, the work is the same every time [2].\n\n# What State Synchronization Asked of a GKR Prover\n\n20 Oct 2026 · 20 min read\n\nIn December 2025 we argued that GKR is a precondition for a state oracle to exist. Building the StateSync-GKR prover showed what an engine needs on top of it: three-state leaves, sequential keys, a fixed 118-layer schedule, sparse sumcheck booking, derived wiring, reused preparation and machine-checked acceptance. Measured end to end, delay moved outside the prover.\n\nGitHub Product site SS-GKR docs arXiv: formal verification arXiv: design and evaluation\n\n## TL;DR\n\n  * In December 2025 we argued that GKR is not an optimization for a state oracle but a precondition for one to exist. Turning that claim into a running prover showed that a precondition does not make an engine. What else the engine needed was taught one item at a time, by the workload and by failures.\n  * State synchronization asks a prover narrow questions: is a record under a published root, is it absent, did a leaf change correctly. What we built barely resembles the January plan. We wrote Rust in place of gnark, and in place of another team's GKR library we implemented sumcheck and GKR to their published specifications on open-source field and hash primitives. The decisions we had not planned outnumbered the ones we had. The January estimates were simulations of a gnark design we never built, and the engine we did build asked different things of us.\n\nWe built it ourselves for two reasons. The first was to keep the verification boundary inside our own code. We wanted to machine-check in Isabelle/HOL that the engine's acceptance coincides with the intended state transition, and to connect that model to the real code. The tool we chose for that connection, Creusot, works on Rust, and the code the connection relies on had to stay under our control. The second reason was the workload.\n\nProving for state synchronization means repeating layered circuits of the same shape, without end, inside the deadline of primary finality. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nsumcheck-gkr-notes/book/04-gkr-protocol.md at main · tmfreiberg/sumcheck-gkr-notes · GitHub (https://github.com/tmfreiberg/sumcheck-gkr-notes/blob/main/book/04-gkr-protocol.md)\nciteturn0search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; In our example, expands to \\begin{equation} \\label{eq-gkr-03} 11 = \\sum_{x\\in{0,1}} \\sum_{y\\in{0,1}} (1-x)y\\big[(6-x)+(6-y)\\big]. \\end{equation} and \\begin{align} \\label{eq-gkr-04} 6 - z &= \\sum_{x_0\\in{0,1}}\\sum_{x_1\\in{0,1}}\\sum_{y_0\\in{0,1}}\\sum_{y_1\\in{0,1}} \\Big{ zx_0(1-x_1)y_0y_1\\big[(-2x_0x_1+x_0-x_1+3)+(-2y_0y_1+y_0-y_1+3)\\big] \\notag \\ &\\qquad\\qquad\\qquad\\qquad\\qquad\\qquad\\qquad\\qquad ... Instead, they sample a uniformly random $\\rho_0\\in\\mathbb{F}^{s_0}$ and check equality at that point: \\begin{equation*} \\widetilde{W}_0(\\rho_0) = \\widetilde{W}_0^(\\rho_0). \\end{equation} If $\\widetilde{W}_0\\neq \\widetilde{W}_0^$, then $\\widetilde{W}_0-\\widetilde{W}_0^$ is a nonzero multilinear polynomial function (degree $\\le s_0$), so by Schwartz-Zippel, \\begin{equation*} \\Pr\\big[\\widetilde{W}_0(\\rho_0)=\\widetilde{W}_0^(\\rho_0)\\big] = \\Pr\\big[(\\widetilde{W}_0-\\widetilde{W}_0^)(\\rho_0)=0\\big] \\le \\frac{s_0}{#\\mathbb{F}}. \\end{equation*} Thus, a match at a random point gives strong evidence that all output gates are correct.\nThe prover's claim is that the true output function $W_0$ equals a purported function $W_0^$ (equivalently, $\\widetilde{W}_0=\\widetilde{W}_0^$ as polynomial functions).\n\nThe verifier does not check equality at all points. Instead, they sample a uniformly random $\\rho_0\\in\\mathbb{F}^{s_0}$ and check equality at that point: \\begin{equation*} \\widetilde{W}_0(\\rho_0) = \\widetilde{W}_0^(\\rho_0). \\end{equation} If $\\widetilde{W}_0\\neq \\widetilde{W}_0^$, then $\\widetilde{W}_0-\\widetilde{W}_0^$ is a nonzero multilinear polynomial function (degree $\\le s_0$), so by Schwartz-Zippel, \\begin{equation*} \\Pr\\big[\\widetilde{W}_0(\\rho_0)=\\widetilde{W}_0^(\\rho_0)\\big] = \\Pr\\big[(\\widetilde{W}_0-\\widetilde{W}_0^)(\\rho_0)=0\\big] \\le \\frac{s_0}{#\\mathbb{F}}. \\end{equation*} Thus, a match at a random point gives strong evidence that all output gates are correct.\n\nFrom Thaler's identity to GKR. Fix a layer $i$. The verifier does not try to check for all $\\mathbf{z}\\in\\mathbb{F}^{s_i}$. Instead, they sample a random point $\\rho_i\\in\\mathbb{F}^{s_i}$ and ask the prover to justify at $\\mathbf{z}=\\rho_i$.\n\nOnce $\\rho_i$ is fixed, the right-hand side of becomes a polynomial function in the $2s_{i+1}$ Boolean variables $(\\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{y})$. The prover and verifier then run sum-check over these variables. At the end of sum-check, the verifier is left with a small number of claimed values of $\\widetilde{W}{i+1}$; the folding step compresses these into a single new claim about $\\widetilde{W}{i+1}$ at a fresh random point $\\rho_{i+1}$.\n\nAs in the sum-check notebook, we distinguish circuit-determined objects from prover-asserted objects. We use $(\\cdot)^$ (e.g. $\\widetilde{W}_i^$) for prover claims, which may be false. For clarity, we sometimes use the notation $;\\overset{?}{=};$ for an equality that may not hold if P is dishonest. Unadorned symbols refer to the true objects induced by the circuit. For readability, we reuse symbols like the verifier challenges $r_j$ and the prover's round polynomials $g_j$ across different sum-check invocations; they are \"reset\" each time a new sum-check begins.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGKR protocol in Expander | Polyhedra Network Documentation (https://docs.polyhedra.network/expander/prover_internals/gkr/)\nciteturn0search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; # GKR protocol in Expander ... It can be combined with a multi-linear polynomial commitment scheme to the circuit input, producing an argument system with linear proving time, linear verification time and sub-linear proof size. ... There, the sumcheck equation looks like the following ... For example, if we're proving 8 copies of the circuit, the variable $r_{simd}, s$r s im d​,s has a size of $\\log_2(8) = 3$lo g 2​(8)=3. ... Reduce $s$s before $y$y to let the prover conclude the new claim $V_i(x, s)$V i​(x,s) first before touching $y$y.\n\n# GKR protocol in Expander\n\nThe GKR protocol is a well-known protocol to prove the satisfiability of layered arithmetic circuits. It reduces a claim about the output layer-by-layer, all the way to a claim about the input. It can be combined with a multi-linear polynomial commitment scheme to the circuit input, producing an argument system with linear proving time, linear verification time and sub-linear proof size.\n\nThe GKR protocol has the advantages of no trusted setup and fast proving time. However, it also has the drawback of relatively large proof size and slow verification time. We make several optimizations in Expander including SIMD execution, batch verificati